I've gone full Aristotle.
Quoting @AmandaAskell
@TheNitrome Forgiveness can be a virtue, but being too liberal with forgiveness can do harm by lowering the bar of acceptable behavior for everyone. Basically, both forgiveness and judgment can become vices if they're employed excessively or with too little discrimination.

Maybe a lot of current debates about LLMs boil down to "was Hume right about imagination?"
Quoting @mark_riedl
@deliprao @AmandaAskell @mmitchell_ai @emilymbender It /might/ be that all reasoning boils down to analogical reasoning (and there is no doubt that humans are very good at it and LLMs are getting good at it). We shall see. My instinct tells me that there is more to the story than that.

In general, I don't think facts about the moral patienthood or agency of one being can depend on facts about the moral patienthood or agency of other beings. It seems important that moral status isn't the kind of property you gain or lose depending on whether others have it.
Quoting @AmandaAskell
@andrewthesmart @rajiinio @o_guest I don't think the problem here is with humanizing AI - it's that you made the value of women conditional on them filling certain roles that AI could in principle fill. If your humanization of women is in any way dependent on not humanizing AI, I think you're not humanizing women.

Since I retweeted my other comment, I'll do the same with this one. I don't think the paper in question is claiming there's a fundamental tension between humanizing women and humanizing anything else - it's discussing harmful ways AIs &amp; robots are currently portrayed as human.
Quoting @AmandaAskell
@o_guest @andrewthesmart @rajiinio @Abebab I took you to be making the stronger claim: that humanizing AI of necessity dehumanizes women, rather than a contingent claim about current practices. I agree that humanizing current AI in the sense of "portraying it as human-like" is often done in ways that are harmful to women.

I never really thought about the fact that we're pretty close to having Harry Potter paintings. Digital displays linked on a network that contain portraits of figures you can interact with, use to send messages to friends, or just let hang out together.
